1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,840
We can see galaxies billions of
light-years away. We can edit DNA.

2
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:09,840
We can build machines that talk,
reason, write music and create images.

3
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:15,160
We can explain lightning, disease,
evolution and the history of the universe.

4
00:00:16,017 --> 00:00:18,827
And yet billions of people
still believe in God.

5
00:00:19,855 --> 00:00:21,055
Why?

6
00:00:23,087 --> 00:00:25,497
Did science simply fail
to replace religion?

7
00:00:26,387 --> 00:00:30,777
Or were we expecting science to answer a
question it was never designed to answer?

8
00:00:33,517 --> 00:00:36,607
For most of human history, the
world was full of mysteries.

9
00:00:37,645 --> 00:00:39,995
A storm wasn't just a physical phenomenon.

10
00:00:40,865 --> 00:00:42,235
It could be a god's anger.

11
00:00:43,162 --> 00:00:45,372
A successful harvest could be a blessing.

12
00:00:46,202 --> 00:00:47,632
A plague could be punishment.

13
00:00:48,679 --> 00:00:51,349
And if you wanted to understand
why something happened,

14
00:00:51,619 --> 00:00:55,729
one obvious explanation was:
Someone wanted it to happen.

15
00:00:56,476 --> 00:00:59,346
Then science started taking
these mysteries apart.

16
00:01:00,263 --> 00:01:02,013
Lightning became electricity.

17
00:01:02,843 --> 00:01:04,593
Disease became biology.

18
00:01:05,563 --> 00:01:07,773
The diversity of life became evolution.

19
00:01:08,747 --> 00:01:10,507
And the universe itself turned out to have

20
00:01:10,587 --> 00:01:13,017
a history stretching
back billions of years.

21
00:01:13,771 --> 00:01:16,171
One by one, things that
once seemed to require

22
00:01:16,251 --> 00:01:19,201
supernatural explanations
got natural ones.

23
00:01:19,989 --> 00:01:22,259
So perhaps religion
should have disappeared.

24
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,690
Except it didn't.

25
00:01:26,981 --> 00:01:31,471
Because explaining how something happens
doesn't necessarily answer why it exists.

26
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:35,240
Science can describe the
evolution of the universe.

27
00:01:36,130 --> 00:01:38,200
But why is there a universe at all?

28
00:01:38,908 --> 00:01:42,278
And science can describe what
happens to the body when we die.

29
00:01:43,148 --> 00:01:45,758
But it can't tell us whether
death has some deeper meaning.

30
00:01:46,537 --> 00:01:49,627
These aren't necessarily
unanswered scientific questions.

31
00:01:50,217 --> 00:01:53,207
Some of them may simply be
different kinds of questions.

32
00:01:54,123 --> 00:01:56,583
And that's one reason
religion can survive even

33
00:01:56,663 --> 00:01:59,513
when its old explanations
of nature disappear.

34
00:02:02,543 --> 00:02:04,873
But there may be something else going on.

35
00:02:05,625 --> 00:02:09,155
Human brains are extremely good
at finding patterns and causes.

36
00:02:10,085 --> 00:02:11,295
That's usually useful.

37
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:15,227
If something moves in the
bushes, assuming there might be

38
00:02:15,307 --> 00:02:18,437
a predator is much safer than
assuming it's just the wind.

39
00:02:19,238 --> 00:02:23,828
If something unexpected happens, we
instinctively ask: What caused this?

40
00:02:24,582 --> 00:02:27,952
And sometimes we go one step
further: Who caused this?

41
00:02:28,731 --> 00:02:31,811
This tendency to detect
agency may be one of the

42
00:02:31,891 --> 00:02:34,961
ingredients that makes
supernatural beliefs intuitive.

43
00:02:35,931 --> 00:02:37,861
The important word here is may.

44
00:02:38,671 --> 00:02:41,641
Human psychology doesn't
prove that God is imaginary.

45
00:02:42,557 --> 00:02:45,047
And here's an even stranger possibility.

46
00:02:45,937 --> 00:02:48,907
What if belief doesn't have
to be conscious to affect us?

47
00:02:49,653 --> 00:02:52,733
In one small experiment,
atheists were asked to read

48
00:02:52,813 --> 00:02:56,043
out statements daring God to
harm the people they loved.

49
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:59,180
They said they didn't
believe a word of it.

50
00:02:59,935 --> 00:03:03,545
But as they spoke, their bodies
showed a measurable stress response.

51
00:03:04,535 --> 00:03:06,625
The same response the believers had.

52
00:03:07,329 --> 00:03:09,839
Their conscious mind
said this meant nothing.

53
00:03:10,049 --> 00:03:12,319
Their bodies seemed less certain.

54
00:03:13,038 --> 00:03:15,688
This doesn't prove
atheists secretly believe.

55
00:03:16,611 --> 00:03:19,031
It shows something
stranger: what we feel and

56
00:03:19,111 --> 00:03:21,321
what we say we believe don't always match.

57
00:03:24,501 --> 00:03:28,571
And then there's death. This might
be the hardest problem of all.

58
00:03:29,314 --> 00:03:31,384
We can understand death scientifically.

59
00:03:32,074 --> 00:03:33,644
Cells stop functioning.

60
00:03:34,454 --> 00:03:35,904
The brain stops working.

61
00:03:36,594 --> 00:03:38,384
The body eventually breaks down.

62
00:03:39,165 --> 00:03:43,355
But understanding the biology doesn't
make the existential problem disappear.

63
00:03:44,275 --> 00:03:46,025
You know that you're going to die.

64
00:03:46,875 --> 00:03:51,345
And, unlike almost every other problem in
life, there is no conventional solution.

65
00:03:52,181 --> 00:03:53,591
Religion offers one.

66
00:03:54,335 --> 00:03:55,925
Maybe death isn't the end.

67
00:03:56,675 --> 00:03:58,285
Maybe consciousness continues.

68
00:03:59,115 --> 00:04:00,555
Maybe there is another world.

69
00:04:00,635 --> 00:04:02,465
Maybe someone is waiting for us.

70
00:04:03,182 --> 00:04:07,292
Those ideas can't simply be dismissed
as an attempt to explain biology.

71
00:04:08,402 --> 00:04:13,052
They're also answers to one of the deepest
emotional problems humans have ever faced.

72
00:04:16,352 --> 00:04:19,702
And religion isn't just a
collection of supernatural claims.

73
00:04:19,892 --> 00:04:22,342
It's also a social system.

74
00:04:23,106 --> 00:04:24,326
Family. Community.

75
00:04:24,406 --> 00:04:28,316
Rituals. Traditions.
Holidays. Marriage. Funerals.

76
00:04:29,028 --> 00:04:30,738
A shared moral framework.

77
00:04:31,508 --> 00:04:34,898
A place where people meet other
people who believe the same things.

78
00:04:35,782 --> 00:04:39,162
A person can inherit a religion
without ever sitting down and

79
00:04:39,242 --> 00:04:43,922
deciding: "After carefully
examining all available evidence, I

80
00:04:44,002 --> 00:04:47,372
have concluded that this particular
theological system is correct."

81
00:04:48,273 --> 00:04:50,283
They may simply grow up with it.

82
00:04:51,035 --> 00:04:53,115
And when millions of people
around you treat something

83
00:04:53,195 --> 00:04:57,005
as normal, it becomes part
of your culture and identity.

84
00:04:57,830 --> 00:04:59,970
That's one reason religion can persist

85
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:02,400
even when individual
beliefs become weaker.

86
00:05:05,170 --> 00:05:07,970
And it isn't only ancient societies — it

87
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:10,620
reaches into how we
imagine the future, too.

88
00:05:11,367 --> 00:05:14,357
Take the sci-fi series
Battlestar Galactica.

89
00:05:15,113 --> 00:05:17,603
Gods... Prophecy... Prayer... Destiny.

90
00:05:18,431 --> 00:05:21,941
And you can even find viewers
dismissing it as "religious crap."

91
00:05:22,751 --> 00:05:24,841
But that's actually what
made it interesting.

92
00:05:25,876 --> 00:05:28,976
Here was a science-fiction
universe filled with spaceships,

93
00:05:29,056 --> 00:05:32,506
artificial intelligence and
technology far beyond ours...

94
00:05:32,976 --> 00:05:34,506
and religion was still there.

95
00:05:35,209 --> 00:05:38,959
Because having better technology
didn't make them stop being human.

96
00:05:39,721 --> 00:05:41,691
And perhaps that's not so unrealistic.

97
00:05:43,001 --> 00:05:47,321
Even if our civilisation becomes vastly
more advanced than it is today, we

98
00:05:47,401 --> 00:05:51,651
might still carry the same questions,
fears and need for meaning with us.

99
00:05:52,363 --> 00:05:56,123
Perhaps our descendants could travel
between stars, create artificial minds

100
00:05:56,203 --> 00:06:00,393
and manipulate matter itself — and
still wonder whether there is a God.

101
00:06:03,423 --> 00:06:08,393
We often imagine the history of humanity
as a march away from superstition.

102
00:06:09,130 --> 00:06:11,380
But reality seems more complicated.

103
00:06:12,341 --> 00:06:15,311
Modernization can make
societies less religious.

104
00:06:16,401 --> 00:06:19,221
And some highly educated,
technologically advanced

105
00:06:19,301 --> 00:06:22,971
societies are among the most
secular places on Earth.

106
00:06:23,767 --> 00:06:28,257
But technological sophistication does
not automatically produce atheism.

107
00:06:29,324 --> 00:06:32,274
And here's something that
doesn't fit the simple story.

108
00:06:33,047 --> 00:06:36,967
In 1914, about forty-two per
cent of American scientists

109
00:06:37,047 --> 00:06:39,997
surveyed said they
believed in a personal God.

110
00:06:40,801 --> 00:06:44,871
In a similar survey in 1997,
the number had barely moved.

111
00:06:45,721 --> 00:06:47,894
About thirty-nine per cent.

112
00:06:48,345 --> 00:06:52,675
But among members of the National Academy
of Sciences, the figure was far lower.

113
00:06:53,605 --> 00:06:55,396
About seven per cent.

114
00:06:55,845 --> 00:06:58,105
So the relationship
between science and belief

115
00:06:58,185 --> 00:07:01,155
isn't nearly as simple as we might expect.

116
00:07:02,064 --> 00:07:05,354
The interesting question isn't
whether scientists believe.

117
00:07:06,164 --> 00:07:09,434
It's why different groups of
scientists believe so differently.

118
00:07:10,290 --> 00:07:14,940
There are religious people who fully
accept evolution and modern cosmology.

119
00:07:15,719 --> 00:07:17,999
So perhaps science and
religion aren't actually

120
00:07:18,079 --> 00:07:20,489
fighting over exactly the same territory.

121
00:07:23,709 --> 00:07:25,269
Of course, this doesn't mean every

122
00:07:25,349 --> 00:07:27,819
religious claim is
compatible with science.

123
00:07:28,797 --> 00:07:33,167
Some religious traditions make very
specific claims about the physical world.

124
00:07:34,057 --> 00:07:36,027
When those claims
contradict well-established

125
00:07:36,177 --> 00:07:39,207
evidence, science has
a way of testing them.

126
00:07:39,861 --> 00:07:42,401
And historically, many
explanations that were once

127
00:07:42,481 --> 00:07:47,031
supernatural have been replaced
by natural ones. That's real.

128
00:07:47,809 --> 00:07:49,589
But it doesn't follow that every question

129
00:07:49,669 --> 00:07:52,999
religion asks is therefore
a scientific question.

130
00:07:53,793 --> 00:07:56,323
And that's where the
argument becomes much harder.

131
00:07:58,773 --> 00:08:00,473
And there's another reason religion may

132
00:08:00,553 --> 00:08:03,703
survive: humans don't
only want explanations.

133
00:08:04,864 --> 00:08:08,154
We want stories. We want
meaning. We want belonging.

134
00:08:09,015 --> 00:08:13,575
We want rituals for the moments when
ordinary life isn't enough. Birth.

135
00:08:13,655 --> 00:08:15,665
Marriage. Death. Grief. Hope.

136
00:08:16,452 --> 00:08:19,172
Even if you don't believe
in God, it's easy to

137
00:08:19,252 --> 00:08:22,222
understand why people might
find these things valuable.

138
00:08:23,119 --> 00:08:25,919
And that makes religion much
harder to eliminate than

139
00:08:25,999 --> 00:08:29,189
simply eliminating an incorrect
explanation of lightning.

140
00:08:32,089 --> 00:08:35,699
So will religion eventually
disappear? Maybe.

141
00:08:36,329 --> 00:08:40,779
Some societies are clearly becoming
more secular. Others aren't.

142
00:08:41,385 --> 00:08:42,865
And we don't have a simple technological

143
00:08:42,945 --> 00:08:46,795
equation where: more
science = less religion.

144
00:08:47,583 --> 00:08:49,743
Human beings aren't
machines that receive new

145
00:08:49,823 --> 00:08:52,773
information and automatically
update their worldview.

146
00:08:53,929 --> 00:08:56,159
We are biological, social creatures.

147
00:08:57,225 --> 00:09:01,035
We inherit beliefs. We fear
death. We search for meaning.

148
00:09:01,931 --> 00:09:05,441
And we tell stories about where we
came from and where we're going.

149
00:09:06,187 --> 00:09:08,777
Science can change what
those stories contain.

150
00:09:09,587 --> 00:09:12,237
It doesn't necessarily
remove our need for stories.

151
00:09:14,997 --> 00:09:17,897
Maybe that's why the question
isn't really: "Why do

152
00:09:17,977 --> 00:09:20,447
people still believe in
God when we have science?"

153
00:09:21,493 --> 00:09:23,793
Maybe the better question
is: "What exactly

154
00:09:23,873 --> 00:09:26,263
were we expecting science to replace?"

155
00:09:27,221 --> 00:09:29,921
If religion was simply a
primitive explanation for thunder

156
00:09:30,001 --> 00:09:34,071
and disease, then science has
already replaced much of it.

157
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:37,040
But if religion is also an
attempt to answer questions

158
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:41,450
about meaning, mortality,
morality and existence itself...

159
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:44,870
then science was never
really playing the same game.

160
00:09:45,632 --> 00:09:48,282
Science can tell us
how the universe works.

161
00:09:49,072 --> 00:09:53,522
It can tell us how stars form, how life
evolves, and how our brains function.

162
00:09:54,327 --> 00:09:56,217
But whether the universe has a purpose...

163
00:09:56,547 --> 00:09:57,897
whether life has meaning...

164
00:09:58,207 --> 00:10:00,757
or whether there is something
beyond what we can observe...

165
00:10:01,087 --> 00:10:03,037
those questions remain open.

166
00:10:03,746 --> 00:10:06,306
And perhaps that's why,
even in an age of artificial

167
00:10:06,386 --> 00:10:09,886
intelligence, genetic engineering
and galaxies photographed

168
00:10:09,966 --> 00:10:13,856
billions of light-years
away, God hasn't disappeared.

169
00:10:17,276 --> 00:10:21,086
Maybe the surprising thing isn't
that humans still believe in God.

170
00:10:21,873 --> 00:10:24,873
Maybe the surprising thing
is that, after thousands of

171
00:10:24,953 --> 00:10:27,783
years, we're still asking
the same enormous questions.
